    "answer": "ppl-kenya-ppb-s1p1-013-option-3",
    "explanation": "IgM is considered the most potent activator of the classical pathway of the complement system due to its unique structure and binding properties:Pentameric Structure: IgM exists as a pentamer, meaning it has five identical antigen-binding sites. This allows it to bind multiple antigens simultaneously, forming a large complex that is highly efficient in initiating the complement cascade. C1q Binding Sites: The pentameric structure of IgM exposes multiple C1q binding sites. C1q is the first component of the classical pathway, and its binding to IgM triggers the cascade. The binding of C1q to IgM leads to a conformational change that activates the C1 complex, initiating a cascade of proteolytic reactions that ultimately result in the formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC- Composed of proteins C5b, C6, C7, C8, and multiple copies of C9). The MAC can then lyse target cells, such as bacteria or infected cells.",
